# wafer-site env config.
#
# Copy to `.env` (gitignored). As committed, `cp .env.example .env && cargo
# run` boots on a fresh database. Native runs read these directly; the
# Cloudflare deploy flow reads CLOUDFLARE_* and IMPRESSPRESS_* from the env
# file it is given (ENV_FILE, default `.env`).

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Native run (cargo run / ./target/release/wafer-site)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

IMPRESSPRESS_DB_PATH=./data/manual/db.sqlite
IMPRESSPRESS_STORAGE_ROOT=./data/manual/storage
IMPRESSPRESS_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8090

# Shared settings. An environment value seeds the variables-table row until
# an admin edits it on the admin Variables page; after that the edit wins.
WAFER_RUN_SHARED__APP_NAME=wafer.run
# This site's real origin: it is also the issuer every access token carries.
WAFER_RUN_SHARED__FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:8090
WAFER_RUN_SHARED__ALLOW_SIGNUP=false
# Turn on once the GitHub OAuth app below is filled in.
WAFER_RUN_SHARED__ENABLE_OAUTH=false
WAFER_RUN__AUTH__REQUIRE_VERIFICATION=true

# First admin, on a fresh database only (ignored once any account exists).
# Set the email AND the password together, or the token alone (then redeem
# it on the /b/auth/bootstrap page with an email and password of your own,
# within 24 hours of the first boot that installs it; later boots leave it
# as it is). Changing the token does not revoke an earlier one that is still
# live. The email alone is refused: the server will not start.
# Use an address you own.
# WAFER_RUN_SHARED__AUTH__BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com
# WAFER_RUN_SHARED__AUTH__BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# WAFER_RUN_SHARED__AUTH__BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# Registry: not read from here. Set these on the admin Variables page once an
# admin exists (they are variables-table rows on every target, see
# src/blocks/registry/config.rs). Both are optional:
#   WAFER_RUN__REGISTRY__ADMIN_EMAIL           the one account that may publish;
#                                              empty = publishing closed
#   WAFER_RUN__REGISTRY__REQUIRED_AUTH_METHOD  e.g. oauth.github; empty = any

# GitHub OAuth (https://github.com/settings/developers). The redirect URI
# is provider-agnostic — the provider is encoded in the signed `state` JWT
# by the single-callback flow at `/b/auth/oauth/callback`.
IMPRESSPRESS__AUTH_UI__OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
IMPRESSPRESS__AUTH_UI__OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
IMPRESSPRESS__AUTH_UI__OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8090/b/auth/oauth/callback

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cloudflare deploy (./scripts/deploy-cloudflare.sh → impresspress deploy)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Keep these in a separate file (e.g. `.env.prod`, gitignored) and pass it
# with ENV_FILE=.env.prod. None of the native settings above reach the
# Worker: on Cloudflare every setting is a row of D1's variables table.
#
# Required:
#   CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID                  — `npx wrangler@4 whoami`
#   CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN                   — Workers, D1, R2 and KV permissions
#   IMPRESSPRESS_CLOUDFLARE_D1_DATABASE_ID — `npx wrangler@4 d1 create <name>`
#   IMPRESSPRESS_DEPLOY_TOKEN              — for a Worker that already holds
#       its deploy token, the same value. A first deploy (no Worker yet)
#       generates one, sets it on the Worker, and prints it once with the
#       `export` line later deploys need; or export your own beforehand.
#
# Optional (override impresspress.toml):
#   IMPRESSPRESS_CLOUDFLARE_WORKER_NAME=wafer-site
#   IMPRESSPRESS_CLOUDFLARE_COMPATIBILITY_DATE=2026-05-01
#   IMPRESSPRESS_CLOUDFLARE_D1_DATABASE_NAME=wafer-site-prod
#   IMPRESSPRESS_CLOUDFLARE_R2_BUCKET_NAME=wafer-site-assets
#
# Worker secrets: `impresspress deploy` sets IMPRESSPRESS_DEPLOY_TOKEN and
# WAFER_RUN__AUTH__JWT_SECRET on the main Worker when it lacks them, each
# from the same-named variable when it is set (so IMPRESSPRESS_DEPLOY_TOKEN
# below becomes the Worker's secret then), otherwise generated. Nothing else
# in this file reaches a Worker secret. The first-admin bootstrap token is
# the operator's own, set before the first deploy onto an empty database and
# deleted once redeemed (deleting or changing it does not revoke an
# unredeemed token, see README.md):
#
#   token=$(openssl rand -hex 32); echo "$token"   # keep it to redeem
#   printf '%s' "$token" | npx wrangler@4 secret put \
#     WAFER_RUN_SHARED__AUTH__BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_TOKEN --name wafer-site
#
# See README.md "Deploy to Cloudflare" for the first-admin steps after it.

CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
IMPRESSPRESS_CLOUDFLARE_D1_DATABASE_ID=
IMPRESSPRESS_DEPLOY_TOKEN=
